=== JOHN BURROW’S INITIAL BRIEF Docket 59475.docx ===
BEFORE THE STATE OFFICE OF ADMINISTRATIVE HEARINGS
PUC DOCKET NO. 59475
SOAH DOCKET NO. 473-26-16349
APPLICATION OF ONCOR ELECTRIC DELIVERY COMPANY LLC AND LCRA TRANSMISSION SERVICES CORPORATION FOR A CERTIFICATE OF CONVENIENCE AND NECESSITY FOR THE BELL COUNTY EAST SWITCH TO BIG HILL 765-kV TRANSMISSION LINE
JOHN BURROW’S INITIAL BRIEF
John Burrow ("Mr. Burrow”) timely files this Initial Brief to the State Office of Administrative Hearings ("SOAH") Administrative Law Judges ("ALJs") regarding Oncor Electric Delivery Company LLC’s (“Oncor’s”) and LCRA Transmission Services Corporation’s (“LCRA’s”)(collectively, “Applicants’”) application for a certificate of convenience and necessity (“CCN”) for Bell County East to Bill Hill 765-kV transmission line.
Mr. Burrow respectfully requests the SOAH ALJs and the Commission to properly and rightfully determine this application does not meet PURA requirements and to therefore find the application must be DENIED. 
I. INTRODUCTION
I, John M. Burrow, Pro Se Intervenor, Party Number 811, Private Landowner, Route Links G71, G72, and G73, ground zero in Bell County Texas, am writing this post hearing initial brief without the benefit of having the certified hearing on merit transcripts. One can say “I’m just winging it”.  I am one of 1,400 landowners that were notified of the subject application after March 26, 2026.  We did not have the opportunity to participate and provide meaningful comment in a public meeting forum.  Over the last two months, I have been forced to navigate in a legal environment with no experience or legal representation, so I ask the SOAH ALJ’s for legal leniency if I am not following format, standards, protocol, or criteria.  My intention is not to degrade your office nor the SOAH.  I have been intimated by the sheer volume of legal requirements being asked of me and a compressed timeline that even the most experienced lawyers cannot manage. Representing myself and my family in a hearing in Austin, listening to lawyers objecting one another, the entering of motions, RFI’s, testimony, exhibits, multiple web sites, and cross examinations of expert witnesses, and neighbors pushing these lines on each other.  All of this while I must continue to work to keep a small business from failing and provide employee’s a way to feed and clothe their families.    
This proceeding concerns one of the largest transmission projects ever proposed in Texas. ALJ’s in other dockets have stated this is the largest and most complex project the SOAH has ever seen.  The Applicants with all their resources of high-powered lawyers, hundreds of experts, and billions of dollars, seek authority to construct 765-kV transmission line infrastructure across hundreds of miles of Texas, thousands of acres of private property, affecting thousands of landowners like me, and imposing billions of dollars of costs upon Texas ratepayers.  They are the ones to prove there is a need. A real need based on accurate data and engineering principles. The only need the Applicants have proven is the need to make billions of dollars off the backs of the ratepayers of Texas. 
I am a proud member of a grassroots statewide coalition of private citizens, farmers, ranchers, country music artist, and state representatives.  We are committed to stand with fellow Texans where big government and powerful corporation have overreached their authority. We are for progress. Build with us not over us! Now, as Paul Harvey says,” Here is the rest of the story”.
The authority sought by Applicants is extraordinary. It includes the power of eminent domain, the ability to permanently burden private property with transmission easements, and the ability to transfer substantial project costs to the citizens of Texas.  Because the authority requested is extraordinary, the burden imposed upon the Applicants is equally significant.
The Applicants must prove compliance with PURA, Commission rules, all statutory requirements, and basic Texas and U.S. constitutional rights afforded to private citizens. They must prove the project is needed. They must demonstrate that the selected routes reasonably balance engineering requirements, landowner impacts, community values, prudent avoidance, geographic diversity, and the public interest. Importantly, they must also demonstrate compliance with the public participation and notification requirements established by the Commission. 
The evidence presented demonstrates that the Applicants failed to satisfy those burdens.
The record establishes significant deficiencies concerning:
Need for the project;
Compliance with 2023 House Bill (HB) 5066 and the 2024 Permian Basin Reliability Plan (PBRP);
Failure to quantify actual power transfer requirements;
Failure to quantify the amount of power Import Path 2 would deliver;
Failure to provide proper notice under 16 TAC §22.52;
Failure to provide meaningful public participation opportunities;
Failure to utilize “current” county tax records;
Failure to adequately identify and evaluate community values;
Failure to implement meaningful prudent avoidance;
Failure to adequately investigate schools, churches, and residences;
Failure to provide meaningful geographic diversity with the top routes of interest that continuously impact the same families;
Failure to accurately identify habitable structures;
Failure to disclose the inclusion of travel trailers as habitable structures;
Failure to exercise adequate quality assurance and quality control;
Failure to demonstrate that Import Path 2 is needed by 2030.
For these reasons, the Administrative Law Judges (ALJ’s) should recommend denial of the Application, and the Commission should ultimately deny the requested Certificate of Convenience and Necessity.
II. SUMMARY OF GROUNDS FOR DENIAL
The evidence supports denial of the Application for the following independent reasons:
A.  Failure to Demonstrate Need
Applicants are required to demonstrate the need as stated in their application for CCN; 
No. 14. “Need for the Proposed Project” to “describe the need for the construction and state how the proposed project will address the need” (Reference Oncor & LCRA TSC Exhibit No. 1, p. 19)
The Applicants failed to establish a quantifiable and actionable need for the project as required by PURA. The Application does not identify:
The specific MW deficiency requiring correction;
The specific MW import requirement at Big Hill;
The specific MW import requirement at Sand Lake;
The amount of power available at Bell County East;
The amount of power expected to flow across Import Path 2;
The amount of reliability improvement provided by Import Path 2.
Without identifying the actual need, Applicants cannot demonstrate that the proposed project appropriately addresses that need. 
B.  Failure to Demonstrate Import Path 2 Is Needed By 2030 and Misrepresentation of 2023 HB 5066
The two key planning documents to justify Import Path 2 is the 2024 PBRP and 2023 HB 5066 as referenced in the application (References Exhibit JG-R-1, HB5066; Exhibit JG-2, PBRP).

The Applicant continues to misrepresent the application need by stating the 2024 Regional Transmission Plan (RTP) or 2025 RTP justifies all three Import Paths are needed by 2030, but the application filed only relies on 2024 PBRP and 2023 HB 5066.  

The 2024 PBRP indicates that Import Path 2 does not become necessary until approximately 2038.
ERCOT reaffirmed this conclusion in its December 12, 2024, Sequencing Letter (Reference JKK Panel Ex 01)
The Applicant misrepresents the objectives of HB5066 in their application by stating the following:
1) extend transmission service to areas where mineral resources have been found;
2) increase available transmission capacity to meet forecasted load; and 
3) provide available infrastructure to reduce interconnection times in areas without access to transmission service.

HB5066 not does state 2) increase available transmission capacity to meet forecasted load.  Instead HB5066 states 2) address increasing available capacity to meet forecasted loads. The Applicants addition of the word “transmission” misrepresents the original objective of HB5066 and creates a monstrous project impacting thousands of private landowners. 
Twenty-five Texas Legislators have reaffirmed in a letter dated May 11, 2026.  The Applicants have misrepresented HB 5066 saying it mandates transmission as the only option to solve the electrification reliability of the Permian Basin (Reference John Burrow Direct Testimony Page 23).   
The application filed on March 26, 2026, contains no persuasive evidence demonstrating that ERCOT's 2038 need determination was ever formally reversed.
C. Failure to Comply with Notice Requirements
Applicants failed to provide meaningful notice and meaningful participation opportunities to approximately 1,400 affected landowners.
Applicants added approximately 400 miles of new route links after public meetings had concluded in June 2025.
These additions dramatically expanded the number of affected properties from approximately 2,800 to approximately 4,200, a thirty-three percent increase.
The Applicant held critical information from the newly affected property owners for six months (Reference JB Exhibit 2)  
Despite these substantial modifications, no additional public meetings were conducted as required by TAC 22.52 (4) (a). 
D. Failure to Comply with TAC 22.52
Applicants failed to satisfy both the letter and intent of 16 TAC §22.52.
The rule exists to ensure affected landowners who are to receive an application receive direct notice and allowed meaningful participation opportunities before route selection decisions become fixed.
Approximately one-third of affected landowners were denied those opportunities.

E. Failure to Properly Evaluate Community Values
Applicants repeatedly testified that public participation meetings are critical for identifying community values. Yet Applicants denied approximately 1,400 landowners any opportunity to participate in those meetings.
Applicants presented little evidence demonstrating that community values were:
Tabulated;
Ranked;
Summarized;
Compared;
Incorporated into route selection.
F. Failure to Implement Prudent Avoidance
Applicants failed to demonstrate meaningful efforts to avoid or reduce impacts upon:
Schools;
Churches;
Residences;
Homesteads;
Community resources;
Areas where people routinely gather.
The record contains numerous examples where Applicants either failed to identify and correctly categorized critical habitable structures such as schools or churches and failed to demonstrate any meaningful effort to avoid them.
G. Failure to Exercise Due Diligence
Applicants failed to conduct adequate field verification and failed to exercise due diligence in identifying:
Schools;
Churches;
Habitable structures;
Future transportation infrastructure;
Community resources;
Geographic diversity concerns.
These deficiencies undermine confidence in the route location study and the resulting recommendations.
H. Failure to Utilize Current Tax Records
The evidence demonstrates Applicants did not consistently utilize current county appraisal district and tax roll information when establishing ownership and mailing addresses.
As a result, as many as 27 affected landowners including Christine Thies and Shawn Hoffman were not notified until June 1, 2026, 67 days after the application was filed thus, they were deprived of notice opportunities required by Commission rules. (Reference Exhibit JKK Panel 10 thru 13) 


I. Failure to Maintain Adequate Quality Assurance and Quality Control
The numerous errors identified throughout this proceeding indicate a lack of adequate QA/QC procedures.
Examples include:
Incorrect ownership information;
Missing ownership information;
Incorrect habitable structure classifications;
Failure to identify schools;
Failure to identify churches;
Inclusion of travel trailers as habitable structures;
Route evaluation deficiencies;
Notification deficiencies.
The cumulative effect of these errors undermines confidence in the Application and supporting studies.
III. APPLICANTS FAILED TO PROVE NEED FOR THE PROJECT PURA §37.056(c)
The burden of proving need rests entirely upon the Applicants.
Landowners are not required to prove the project is unnecessary.
Rather, Applicants must affirmatively demonstrate both: 1) a quantifiable need; and 2) that the proposed project addresses that need.
Applicants failed to satisfy either requirement.
A. The Application Relies Entirely Upon 2023 HB 5066 and the 2024 PBRP
Applicants' need case relies almost exclusively upon:
2023 HB 5066; and
ERCOT's 2024 PBRP.
Neither document independently establishes the need for this specific project.
HB 5066 authorized development of a plan to address reliability concerns in the Permian Basin.
HB 5066 did not mandate construction of Import Path 2.
HB 5066 did not mandate construction of a Bell County East to Big Hill transmission line.
HB 5066 did not require transmission-only solutions.
Instead, HB 5066 required consideration of methods such as generation alternatives to address increasing capacity needs.
These facts are substantiated by the previously mentioned Texas State Legislators letter, dated May 11, 2026. 
ERCOT’s 2024 RBPB states Import Paths 2 need starts to appear in 2038, a need twelve years in the future. 

B. Applicants Failed to Quantify Actual Need
Applicants never identify:
The specific MW deficit at Big Hill;
The specific MW deficit at Sand Lake;
The amount of import power required;
The amount of import power available;
The amount of power expected to flow on Import Path 2.
The Application repeatedly references future load growth but never translates those forecasts into a specific, actionable need capable of supporting construction of this project.
The Commission cannot determine whether the proposed solution is appropriately sized when the underlying need has never been quantified.
The Applicants only response when questioned is HB 5066 mandates the use of transmission (a misrepresentation) and ERCOT came up with the numbers when in fact the transmission providers provide those numbers.
C. Applicants Failed to Demonstrate the Project's Ability to Address Any Quantified Need
The Application also fails to explain:
How much power would flow through Import Path 2;
When that power would flow;
Where that power originates;
Whether Bell County East has sufficient available generation;
Whether transmission constraints elsewhere would limit delivery.
Without these fundamental facts, the Commission cannot determine whether the proposed project would solve the problem Applicants claim exists.
D. Applicants' Bi-Directional Flow Assertions Are Unsupported
Applicants assert the project will improve bi-directional power flow.
However:
HB 5066 does not identify bi-directional flow as a project objective;
The 2024 PBRP does not identify bi-directional flow as a project objective;
The project is title as “Import Path” from East Bell County to Big Hill;
The Application contains no engineering analysis validating this claim.
The reference appears unsupported and cannot substitute for proof of need.
E. Recent Evidence Undermines the Load Forecasts Supporting the Project
Evidence presented during the hearing demonstrates that significant reductions have occurred in projected load growth assumptions relied upon by ERCOT.
The Commission should carefully evaluate whether the forecasts supporting Import Path 2 remain valid.
If the underlying forecasts are materially overstated, then the need determination supporting the project is similarly undermined.
IV. IMPORT PATH 2 IS NOT NEEDED UNTIL APPROXIMATELY 2038
The strongest evidence regarding timing comes directly from ERCOT.
ERCOT's 2024 Permian Basin Reliability Plan indicates that Import Path 2 need does not start to appear until 2038.
ERCOT subsequently reinforced that conclusion through its December 12, 2024, Sequencing Letter.
In that letter, ERCOT Vice President Ms. Kristi Hobbs stated that projects needed to serve forecasted 2030 load should be prioritized ahead of projects needed to serve forecasted 2038 load.
ERCOT’s Ms. Kristi Hobbs further advised that only two of the three proposed 765-kV import paths were needed by 2030.
Import Path 2 was not identified as one of those priority projects.
Ms. Hobbs appeared before the Commission during the December 19, 2024, open meeting and was available to answer questions concerning the Sequencing Letter. The Commission open meeting public record contains no evidence that ERCOT formally withdrew or changed the Sequencing Letter. 
The 2024 RTP was released on Dec 20, 2026, one day after the Commission’s open meeting on December 19, 2024.  The Applicants claim appears to be that ERCOT changed their load findings in one day on a very large critical study that takes months to develop resulting in negative impacts to thousands of acres of Texas farms and ranches and thousands of hardworking Texans.  This does not add up.  
Consequently, the most credible planning evidence before the Commission demonstrates that Import Path 2 is not required in 2030 and that HB5066 does not mandate import path 2.
Given the impacts to landowners associated with this project, the Commission should not authorize eminent domain and burden billions of dollars of infrastructure costs on Texas ratepayers based upon a speculative future requirement that ERCOT determined does not materialize for twelve years.
V. FAILURE TO PROVIDE REQUIRED PUBLIC NOTICE AND MEANINGFUL PUBLIC PARTICIPATION PURA §37.054, PURA §37.056, and 16 TAC §22.52(a)(4)
The Commission's routing process depends upon meaningful public participation. Public participation cannot occur when affected landowners are not informed that a project exists.
The evidence demonstrates and Applicants admit by their on testimony they expanded the project after the June 2025 public meetings yet failed to provide the 1,400 newly affected landowners the same participation opportunities afforded to earlier landowners.
This failure undermined the integrity of the routing process and deprived approximately 1,400 landowners of the opportunity to provide information concerning community values, routing constraints, prudent avoidance considerations, and alternative route locations.


A. Public Meetings Were Conducted Before the Project Was Fully Defined
Applicants conducted public meetings during June 2025.  At that time, approximately 2,800 property owners were identified as affected landowners that would receive notice of application were invited to participate.
Applicants repeatedly testified throughout this proceeding that public meetings serve a critical function in route development and route refinement.
Applicants represented that public meetings were intended to:
Gather community value information;
Identify routing constraints;
Obtain local knowledge;
Identify residences and sensitive receptors;
Identify schools and churches;
Evaluate route alternatives;
Improve route selection.
The Commission's rules recognize the importance of these public meetings and require them because public participation is a necessary component of the routing process.
However, the project presented in the March 26, 2026, Application was materially different from the project presented during the June 2025 public meetings. Over 400 miles of new alternative routes were added.
B. Applicants Expanded the Project Scope After Public Meetings
The evidence demonstrates that after completion of the June 2025 public meetings, Applicants:
Removed approximately 140 miles of route links;
Modified approximately 68 miles of route links;
Added approximately 400 miles of entirely new route links;
Increased affected properties from approximately 2,800 to approximately 4,200.
These changes are not minor adjustments; they fundamentally altered the project and greatly expanded the number of affected landowners.
The addition of approximately 400 miles of route links represents one of the most significant route modifications in any transmission routing proceeding.
Yet despite these substantial changes, Applicants conducted no additional public meetings, did not notify PUC staff, only stating they exceeded what was required by code (Reference JB Exhibit 2). 
C. Approximately 1,400 Landowners Were Never Afforded the Opportunity to Attend a Public Meeting
The evidence demonstrates that approximately 1,400 ultimately affected property owners never received notice of a public meeting.
These landowners never had the opportunity to:
Meet with project representatives;
Ask questions;
Review route alternatives;
Identify routing constraints;
Provide local knowledge;
Identify community values;
Suggest alternative routes;
Retain legal counsel during the preliminary stages of the project;
As a result, approximately one-third of all affected landowners were denied opportunities afforded to every landowner notified during the June 2025 public meetings.
The Commission cannot reasonably conclude that public participation requirements were satisfied when one-third of affected landowners never had the opportunity to participate.
D. Applicants Knew of These Additional Landowners Long Before Filing the Application
The evidence indicates Applicants had knowledge of the 400 miles of newly developed alternative route links six months before filing the Application.
Despite this knowledge, Applicants:
Did not notify the newly 1,400 affected landowners;
Did not conduct additional public meetings;
Did not provide meaningful outreach;
Did not provide equal participation opportunities
Did not inform the PUC staff.
Applicants cannot simultaneously argue that that they exceeded what was required and that public participation is critical while withholding participation opportunities from approximately 1,400 affected landowners.
Both positions cannot be true.
If public participation is important enough to require public meetings, then all affected landowners must be given a meaningful opportunity to participate.
E. Late Notice Further Prejudiced Newly Affected Landowners
The prejudice suffered by newly affected landowners continued after filing of the Application.  Twenty-seven or more landowners may not have been notified weeks to months after the application was filed 
The record contains evidence that certain landowners, including Christine Thies and Shane Hoffman, did not receive notification of the Application until June 1, 2026 (Reference JKK Panel 10 thru 13).
This occurred approximately 67 days after the March 26, 2026, filing of the Application.
By that time:
Procedural deadlines were already approaching;
Discovery schedules were underway;
Expert witnesses had been retained by other parties;
Significant strategic decisions had already occurred.
These landowners entered the proceeding months behind parties who had received earlier notice.


F. Lack of Notice Severely Limited Access to Counsel
The consequences of delayed notice extended beyond procedural deadlines.
1,400 Landowners were not afforded an opportunity of a public meeting, they became aware of this project only after receiving the notice of the application after March 26, 2026, faced substantial difficulty or was not able to obtain qualified legal representation.
All attorneys with specialized knowledge of transmission line litigation like Mr. Bayliff and Mr. Brady, had already been retained by other parties and the parties who had been notified months earlier.
Landowners who received notice months later entered the process at a significant disadvantage through no fault of their own.
This disparity is precisely why meaningful notice is required before an application 
VI. APPLICANTS FAILED TO COMPLY WITH THE INTENT OF 16 TAC §22.52(a)(4)
Applicants have repeatedly argued that compliance with 16 TAC §22.52(a)(4) was satisfied because public meetings were conducted in June 2025. This argument ignores the broader purpose and intent of the rule.
Applicant direct testimony from Ms. Casey D. Petty indicates either a misunderstanding or another misrepresentation of code.  

In her direct testimony, Ms. Petty was asked “DID THE APPLICANTS HOLD A PUBLIC PARTICIPATION MEETING BEFORE FILING THE APPLICATION? “ 

Ms. Petty’s answer was “Yes. As discussed in Sections 2.5 and 5.0 of the Environmental Assessment and Routing Study, the Applicants hosted three public open-house meetings for the proposed transmission line project.  Because at least 25 landowners were entitled to receive notice based on the preliminary alternative routes, a public meeting was required under 16 TAC §22.52(a)(4)”.  
Ms. Petty’s answer makes one to believe that only landowners on preliminary alternative routes that were entitled to receive direct mail notice of the application are afforded the opportunity to participate in a public meeting.  This is furthest from the truth. 
The code makes no distinction between the 2,800 landowners on preliminary alternative routes invited to the public meeting and the 1,400 landowners on the newly added alternative routes not invited to the public meeting regardless of the date a route is added.  
16 TAC §22.52(a)(4) is clear, “The utility must hold at least one public meeting prior to the filing if 25 or more persons would be entitled to receive direct mail notice of the application.”   
Both the 1,400 and 2,800 landowners are entitled to receive direct mail notice of the application and thus both, by code, are afforded the opportunity to participate in a public meeting.  The Applicant chose not to abide.   
16 TAC §22.52(a)(4) states “The utility must hold at least one public meeting.  This wording supports the situation we are now in.   It provides the flexibility to hold additional public meetings to support the addition of new alternative routes with landowners numbering more than 25.  If this was not the case, the code would simply state “must hold one public meeting”.
The ALJ’s and Commission must not view this as a check the box rule and require the Applicant to adhere to this important and critical rule.
The Commission and the codes exist to ensure affected Texans have a meaningful opportunity to participate before routing decisions are finalized.
A. The Purpose of The Rule Is Public Participation
The scope of the project expanded to include 1,400 new affected landowners.  
16 TAC §22.52(a)(4) purpose is clear, “The utility must hold at least one public meeting prior to the filing if 25 or more persons would be entitled to receive direct mail notice of the application.”  
The rule is not to create disparity among landowners but rather to create meaningful opportunity to participate for all landowners.
The new 1,400 landowners on the added 400 miles of new alternative routes would be entitled to receive direct mail notice of the application and thus should have been afforded the opportunity to participate in one public meeting.
The rule recognizes that meaningful public participation is essential when routing major transmission projects.
Public meetings are not a box to be checked. Public meetings are the mechanism through which landowners provide:
Community value information;
Routing constraints;
School locations;
Church locations;
Future development plans;
Local knowledge.
When approximately 1,400 landowners never receive that opportunity, the purpose of the rule has not been achieved.
B. Applicants' Interpretation Produces an Absurd Result
Under Applicants' interpretation:
Public meetings could be conducted for one set of landowners but not others;
Hundreds of miles of new route links could later be added;
Thousands of additional acres could become affected;
More than 1,400 new landowners could become impacted;
No additional public outreach would ever be required.
This interpretation defeats the purpose of the rule and eliminates meaningful public participation for newly affected landowners.
The ALJ’s and Commission should reject that interpretation.
C. The Record Demonstrates Actual Harm
The record demonstrates actual harm.
Approximately 1,400 landowners:
Lost the opportunity to attend public meetings;
Lost the opportunity to provide routing information;
Lost the opportunity to identify community values;
Lost the opportunity to influence route development;
Lost months of preparation time;
Lost access to available legal resources.
These harms are real, measurable, and directly attributable to Applicants' failure to provide equivalent participation opportunities.
D. Relief for the 1,400 Landowners Is Appropriate
The Commission has the authority to ensure fairness and protect the public interest.  Landowners who were denied participation opportunities should receive equivalent relief.
The evidence supports a finding that newly affected landowners lost approximately 206 days of participation opportunity compared to those invited to the June 2025 public meetings.
VII. DUE PROCESS CONCERNS
The United States Constitution and Texas Constitution guarantee fundamental due process protections.
Among those protections are:
Notice;
Opportunity to be heard;
Meaningful participation;
Opportunity to present evidence;
Opportunity to challenge evidence.
Those protections become especially important when governmental action may ultimately result in the taking of private property through eminent domain.
When affected landowners are not provided timely notice and meaningful participation opportunities, due process concerns necessarily arise.
The Commission should not authorize a project of this magnitude where substantial numbers of affected Texans were denied the same participation opportunities afforded to others.
VIII. COMMUNITY VALUES PURA §37.056(c)(4)(A)
Community values are one of the express routing factors the Commission is required to consider when determining whether a proposed transmission line route is in the public interest.
Applicants repeatedly testified that public participation meetings were an essential component of identifying community values and obtaining local information necessary for route selection.
Applicants' own Environmental Assessment and Route Location Study state that public outreach was intended to Identify community values; Identify local concerns; Identify routing constraints; Gather local knowledge; Improve route selection.
Despite repeatedly emphasizing the importance of public participation, Applicants denied approximately 1,400 affected landowners the opportunity to participate in those meetings.
As a result, a substantial portion of the community value information that should have been considered was never collected.
The Commission cannot reasonably conclude that community values were adequately identified and considered when approximately one-third of affected landowners never had an opportunity to provide them.
A. Applicants Failed to Demonstrate Community Values Were Actually Used
While Applicants repeatedly referenced community values throughout this proceeding, the record contains little evidence demonstrating how community value information was incorporated into route selection.
The evidence demonstrates that community value information may have been collected from some landowners, but Applicants failed to show how that information was systematically evaluated and incorporated into the route development process.
The Commission should give little weight to generalized statements that community values were considered when the record contains no meaningful demonstration of how they were evaluated.
B. Community Values Cannot Be Fully Evaluated When One-Third of Affected Landowners Are Excluded
Applicants' route development process excluded approximately 1,400 affected landowners from public participation opportunities.
Those landowners were denied the ability to provide information regarding:
Existing residences;
Family-owned ranches;
Agricultural operations;
Schools;
Churches;
Community gathering areas;
Planned developments;
Recreational uses;
Conservation efforts.
Because this information was never collected from a substantial portion of affected landowners, the resulting community value analysis is necessarily incomplete.
IX. GEOGRAPHIC DIVERSITY PURA §37.056(c)(4)
The Commission has long recognized that geographic diversity is an important routing consideration because it prevents the repeated concentration of transmission impacts on the same families, properties, communities, and public resources.
The evidence demonstrates that Applicants failed to meaningfully apply geographic diversity principles to Route Links G71, G72, and G73.
Instead of spreading impacts across the broader study area, Applicants repeatedly concentrated impacts on the same landowners, the same residences, the same agricultural operations, and the same community resources.
A. New Alternate Route Links G71, G72, And G73 Repeatedly Impact the Same Families
The evidence demonstrates that new alternative Route Links G71, G72, and G73 repeatedly affect the same properties and families throughout the route development process.
Rather than creating meaningful route separation and geographic diversity, these route links repeatedly converge upon the same affected landowners.
The same residences are repeatedly impacted; the same agricultural operations are repeatedly impacted; the same community resources are repeatedly impacted; the same property owners bear a disproportionate share of project burdens. This is the opposite of geographic diversity.
These same landowners are among the 1,400 landowners that were not invited to a public meeting. 
If they were afforded this opportunity, they would have provided comment on geographic diversity concerns.
The Commission should recognize this unfairness could have been resolved if these landowners had the opportunity to participate. 
B. Route Links G71, G72 And G73 Dominate the Applicants' Preferred Routes
The record demonstrates that Route Links G71, G72 and G73 are heavily represented throughout Applicants' route analysis.
Specifically:
G72 and G73 are included within Route 894, the Applicants' Best Meets Route;
G72 and G73 are included within Route 487, PUC Staff Recommendation;
G71 and G73 are included within Route 392, Top Eight Routes of Interest
G72 and G73 appear in all six of Applicants' Top Six Routes;
G72 and G73 appear in twenty-seven of Applicants' Top Thirty Routes;
G72 and G73 appear in ninety-two of the one hundred twenty-two routes identified in the Application.
These statistics demonstrate that the Applicants repeatedly relied upon the same route links while simultaneously claiming geographic diversity was considered.
A route segment appearing in ninety-two of one hundred twenty-two routes cannot reasonably be characterized as geographically diverse.
Instead, it represents a repeated concentration of impacts on the same area and the same landowners.
C. Geographic Diversity Was Not Meaningfully Applied
The overwhelming presence of G71, G72 and G73 throughout the route inventory demonstrates that geographic diversity was not a meaningful factor in route development.
Had geographic diversity been genuinely considered:
Alternative corridors would have been developed;
Impacts would have been distributed more broadly;
Additional route segments would have been evaluated;
The same landowners would not repeatedly appear throughout route combinations.
The evidence demonstrates the opposite occurred.
The repeated use of G71, G72, and G73 throughout the route inventory suggests that alternative corridors were not given meaningful consideration.
Applicants effectively treated these route links as predetermined building blocks of their preferred routing solutions.


X. ROUTE LINK G71 CREATE DISPROPORTIONATE IMPACTS
The evidence demonstrates that Route Link G71 impose significant burdens upon affected landowners while failing to provide meaningful routing advantages that justify those impacts.
A. Route Link G71 Bisects Parcel 5366
Route Link G71 traverses Parcel 5366 and effectively divides the property into separate sections.
Route Link G71 generally parallels an existing utility corridor, that fact does not eliminate the additional burden imposed by a new 765-kV transmission easement.
The cumulative effect of the existing utility easement and the proposed 765-kV transmission easement creates a substantial burden upon the property.
B. Approximately Fifteen Acres Would Be Isolated
The combined effect of Route Link G71 and the existing utility easement would isolate approximately fifteen acres of land. This isolated acreage would become significantly impaired.
The isolated tract would experience diminished utility for:
Residential development;
Agricultural use;
Commercial development;
Recreational use;
Future planning opportunities.
The resulting configuration would leave a portion of the property with no practical utility.
XI. PRUDENT AVOIDANCE, PURA §37.056(c)(4)(A), 16 TAC §25.101(a)(3), 16 TAC §25.101(a)(6), 16 TAC §25.101(b)(3)(B)(iv)
The Commission's routing rules require consideration of prudent avoidance and minimization of impacts to people and sensitive land uses whenever reasonable alternatives exist.
§25.101(a)(6) Prudent avoidance is defined by “the limiting of exposures to electric and magnetic fields that can be avoided with reasonable investments of money and effort”. It is not to be taken lightly.  The implementation of this rule should be taken seriously during route development.   The application does not provide any reliable information that prudent avoidance was seriously used.   Community gatherings such as Schools, home schools, and churches should have been prudently avoided
Cross examination testimony from the Applicants and other party expert witnesses support this by stating human exposure and not habitable structure exposure is the priority.  
However, the evidence demonstrates that Applicants failed to conduct the level of investigation necessary to support a finding that prudent avoidance was meaningfully applied.



A. Applicants Failed to Identify Numerous Areas of Concern
The record demonstrates that Applicants failed to accurately identify or describe several sensitive land uses located along proposed routes.
Examples include:
Florence Elementary School;
First Cedar Valley Baptist Church
Lawler Baptist Church;
Holy Annunciation Orthodox Church;
Homeschools;
Newly constructed residences;
Community gathering locations.
These errors demonstrate a fundamental lack of understanding of the communities affected by the project. Meaningful prudent avoidance cannot occur when the route developer does not know what is being avoided.
B. Florence Elementary School
